# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked. Grafana Labs
# publishes a `grafana` Helm chart with some two hundred values, a sidecar model for dashboards and
# datasources, and an image tag rather than a digest; what is wanted here is one Deployment from a
# pinned image with a provisioning file this platform renders, so the four objects are written here and
# the only things taken from upstream are the container image and one plugin.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/grafana/grafana

# The image this chart runs, unmodified.
image: docker.io/grafana/grafana
image-source: https://github.com/grafana/grafana

# ── LICENCE — ADR-011, read for a DEPLOYED component ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ AGPL-3.0, AND THE ROW IN ADR-011 § The licence audit IS THE DECISION: "Grafana | AGPL-3.0 |
# ⚠ Offerable as a managed instance (we distribute, we do not modify). Our portal must not embed or link
# Grafana code — it embeds rendered dashboards by URL." Three readings, each checked against this chart:
#
#   1. DISTRIBUTED, NOT MODIFIED. The Deployment runs grafana/grafana by digest, straight from Docker
#      Hub, configured the way upstream documents — GF_* environment variables and a datasource
#      provisioning file. No image is built, no file inside one is patched, no Grafana library is
#      linked into any platform assembly. AGPL's network clause binds whoever MODIFIES and serves; this
#      platform serves upstream's bytes, and a tenant who wants the source is sent to upstream's.
#   2. NO GRAFANA CODE IN THE PORTAL. The `url` action hands back an address, and
#      GF_SECURITY_ALLOW_EMBEDDING=true lets a rendered panel sit in an iframe by URL — the one
#      integration the row permits. No Grafana package is in portal/package.json and
#      GrafanaDeclarationTests keeps it that way.
#   3. THE BUILD GATE'S ALLOW-LIST IS FOR THE BUNDLE, AND THIS IS NOT A BUNDLE COMPONENT.
#      build/Build.Licence.cs scans charts/bundle/ and the platform's own images; a chart under
#      charts/managed/ that renders an upstream image — haproxy before this, the collector beside it —
#      is outside both scans. The exception ADR-011 § Enforcement asks for is written into
#      LicenceExceptions NOW, keyed by this image and carrying this reading as its argument, so the day
#      the scan widens to workload images it finds the argument and not a red row. conformance.yaml
#      § owed, `licence-scan-does-not-read-workload-images`.
#
# Had the row refused AGPL for a deployed component, the type would still be published and
# GrafanaReconciler would fail every pass naming the ADR. The row does not refuse.
licence: AGPL-3.0, run as an unmodified upstream image in its own container; ADR-011 § Grafana

# The one plugin, installed by Grafana's own in-process installer (GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_SYNC) before the
# server listens. ⚠ NOT by grafana-cli, which is what this file said until the adversarial review of #32
# ran the image: at 13.2.2 run.sh logs GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS as deprecated and does nothing with it unless
# GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS_FORCE=true; the server reads the variable itself and folds it into the same
# preinstall list. § What was run, below, is the transcript.
plugin: grafana-clickhouse-datasource@4.21.3
plugin-source: https://github.com/grafana/clickhouse-datasource
# ⚠ Apache-2.0 — its LICENSE at v4.21.3 read on 2026-09-17. Fetched from grafana.com on every pod start
# and not pinned by digest; conformance.yaml § owed, `clickhouse-plugin-is-fetched-at-start`.
plugin-licence: Apache-2.0

# ── The pin, and how it was resolved ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ BY DIGEST, IN THE BUNDLE'S SHAPE. Resolved on 2026-09-17:
#
#   $ docker buildx imagetools inspect grafana/grafana:13.2.2
#   Name:      docker.io/grafana/grafana:13.2.2
#   MediaType: application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json
#   Digest:    sha256:ac461fb352abc50da10a51c7d02462e9c05488f11f53f14b3ad79a8145f638a0
#
# The manifest LIST digest, so amd64 and arm64 nodes pull through one reference. 13.2.2 was
# `latest` when resolved (published 2026-09-15); the same pin is Grafanas.Image in C# and `image` in
# values.yaml, and GrafanaDeclarationTests compares the two.
#
# ── What was read off the image, and where it landed ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   * `User: 472` — numeric. Written into the pod's securityContext as runAsUser, runAsGroup and
#     fsGroup (Grafanas.GrafanaUid), the last so the emptyDir under /var/lib/grafana is writable.
#   * `Entrypoint: /run.sh`, `ExposedPorts: 3000/tcp`. Grafana's own entrypoint; nothing is overridden.
#   * No `org.opencontainers.image.licenses` label — only `org.opencontainers.image.source`. The
#     licence is read off the repository's LICENSE (AGPL-3.0) and not off the image.
#
# ── What was RUN, and what it decided ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS CHART READ THE IMAGE'S CONFIG AND GRAFANA'S DOCUMENTATION AND NEVER RAN
# THE IMAGE. The adversarial review of #32 did, and found a pod that goes Ready with its default
# datasource dead. Every fact below is from `docker run` of the pinned digest on 2026-09-18, under the
# rendered Deployment's constraints (`--read-only`, tmpfs on /tmp and /var/lib/grafana, `--user 472:472`,
# `--cap-drop ALL`, the rendered datasources.yaml mounted read-only, the three CYBERCLOUD_* variables set).
#
#   1. /run.sh, read out of the image: GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS is deprecated and inert.
#
#        >&2 echo "WARN: GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS is deprecated. Use GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL or
#                  GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_SYNC instead. ..."
#        if [ "${GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS_FORCE}" = "true" ]; then ... grafana cli ... plugins install ...
#
#      grafana-cli runs only under the FORCE flag. Without it the SERVER reads the variable and puts the
#      plugin on its own preinstall list, so "installed by grafana-cli at pod start" was false.
#
#   2. conf/defaults.ini § [plugins]: `preinstall_auto_update = true` — "preinstalled plugins without a
#      pinned version will be updated to the latest version" — and `bundled_plugins = data/plugins-bundled`.
#      /usr/share/grafana/data/plugins-bundled/ holds prometheus, loki, tempo, elasticsearch, influxdb,
#      jaeger, zipkin, mysql, mssql, grafana-postgresql-datasource, opentsdb, stackdriver and
#      grafana-pyroscope-datasource: at 13.x the Prometheus datasource is a bundled PLUGIN on the root
#      filesystem, not code compiled into the server.
#
#   3. With GF_INSTALL_PLUGINS="grafana-clickhouse-datasource 4.21.3" and nothing else (the first
#      version of this chart), on the read-only root:
#
#        logger=plugin.installer msg="Updating plugin" pluginId=prometheus from=13.1.7 to=13.1.9
#        logger=plugin.prometheus msg="plugin process exited"
#        level=error msg="Failed to install plugin" pluginId=prometheus
#          error="unlinkat /usr/share/grafana/data/plugins-bundled/prometheus: read-only file system"
#
#        GET /api/health                                   → 200 {"database":"ok","version":"13.2.2"}
#        GET /api/datasources/uid/cybercloud-metrics/health → 404 {"messageId":"plugin.notRegistered",
#                                                                 "message":"Plugin not registered"}
#        GET /api/plugins?type=datasource                  → no "prometheus" in the list
#
#      The readiness probe is /api/health. It answered 200 the whole time.
#
#   4. With GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_DISABLED=true instead: Prometheus alive, ClickHouse never installed —
#      "It has the same effect as setting preinstall to an empty list", and that list is where the
#      synchronous entry goes too:
#
#        GET /api/datasources/uid/cybercloud-logs/health → 404 plugin.notRegistered
#
#   5. What the chart renders now — GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_SYNC=grafana-clickhouse-datasource@4.21.3 and
#      GF_PLUGINS_PREINSTALL_AUTO_UPDATE=false — on the read-only root, 25 s after /api/health:
#
#        logger=plugin.backgroundinstaller msg="Installing plugins"
#          plugins="[{ID:grafana-clickhouse-datasource Version:4.21.3 URL:}]"
#        logger=installer.fs msg="Downloaded and extracted grafana-clickhouse-datasource v4.21.3 zip
#          successfully to /var/lib/grafana/plugins/grafana-clickhouse-datasource"
#        (no "Updating plugin" line for any bundled plugin)
#
#        GET /api/datasources/uid/cybercloud-metrics/health → 400 {"message":"Post
#          \"https://telemetry.cybercloud.svc/select/123/prometheus/api/v1/query\": dial tcp: lookup
#          telemetry.cybercloud.svc ... no such host - There was an error returned querying the
#          Prometheus API.","status":"ERROR"}
#        GET /api/datasources/uid/cybercloud-logs/health    → 400 {"message":"[network] failed to query
#          server hello: ... Post \"https://telemetry.cybercloud.svc:443/sql/ws_abc?...&database=ws_abc
#          ...\": dial tcp: lookup telemetry.cybercloud.svc ... no such host","status":"ERROR"}
#
#      Both plugins alive, both requests carrying the substituted accountID (123) and database (ws_abc),
#      both failing on DNS because the probe has no VictoriaMetrics and no ClickHouse — which is the
#      failure a working datasource has in that container. The same two questions, at a workspace's
#      real coordinates, are GrafanaClusterBackedConformance.TheGrafanaPodStartsAndBothDatasourcesAnswer.
#      ⚠ Grafana's default preinstall list (grafana-lokiexplore-app, grafana-pyroscope-app,
#      grafana-exploretraces-app, grafana-metricsdrilldown-app, grafana-advisor-app) still downloads in
#      the background into /var/lib/grafana/plugins; none of them is a datasource and none is this
#      product.
#
#   6. The same env set with `--network none`: exit 1 in three seconds, before the server listens.
#
#        logger=plugin.backgroundinstaller level=error msg="Failed to install plugins"
#          error="failed to install plugin grafana-clickhouse-datasource@4.21.3: Get
#          \"https://grafana.com/api/plugins/grafana-clickhouse-datasource/versions\": ... network is
#          unreachable"
#        logger=backgroundsvcs.managerAdapter level=error msg="Module failed"
#          module=*authimpl.UserAuthTokenService err="... depends on module plugin.backgroundinstaller,
#          which has failed ..."
#
#      So "a cluster with no egress starts a Grafana with one datasource of two", which the first version
#      of conformance.yaml asserted from documentation, is also false: it starts nothing, and the pod
#      crash-loops naming grafana.com. The louder failure is the one kept.
#
#   7. GET /api/datasources/uid/{uid}/health with GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ENABLED=true and no credential: 400
#      for both, the same bodies as 5 — an anonymous Viewer may ask a datasource's health. That is how
#      the cluster-backed test asks through the API server's service proxy, which strips the
#      Authorization header it authenticated this process with before forwarding.
#
# Read from Grafana's documentation (Provisioning § Data sources, Configuration § security and
# auth.anonymous) at 13.x:
#
#   * `$VAR` and `${VAR}` are expanded in every provisioning-file value at load; `$$` is a literal.
#     That is what lets the file carry no coordinate of the workspace.
#   * `GF_SECURITY_ALLOW_EMBEDDING=true` removes `X-Frame-Options: deny`; without it a panel URL in an
#     iframe renders nothing. It is unconditional here because embedding by URL is the only way this
#     platform will ever show a Grafana panel (ADR-011).
#   * `GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ENABLED` / `GF_AUTH_ANONYMOUS_ORG_ROLE=Viewer` is the `anonymousViewers`
#     property; off, every visit signs in.
#   * The datasource `uid`s are fixed (`cybercloud-metrics`, `cybercloud-logs`) so a dashboard JSON a
#     tenant exports from one instance imports into another.
#
# Read from grafana/clickhouse-datasource's README at v4.21.3: jsonData carries `host`, `port`,
# `protocol` (native|http), `secure`, `path`, `defaultDatabase`, `username`; secureJsonData carries
# `password`. The HTTP protocol with a path is what lets the datasource speak to the workspace's SQL
# endpoint — MonitorWorkspaces.SqlEndpoint, `https://telemetry.cybercloud.svc/sql/{database}` — rather
# than to a ClickHouse host this chart would otherwise have to name.
